Derive __BITS_PER_LONG from the compiler, not from __x86_64__

x86's UAPI header decides the word size from __x86_64__ alone and says 32
otherwise. The wasm64 target this tree builds for is LP64 but defines
neither __i386__ nor __x86_64__, so it would take the 32-bit branch and
every UAPI type built on __kernel_long_t would come out the wrong width.

Dropping the branch hands the decision to asm-generic/bitsperlong.h,
which computes it from __CHAR_BIT__ * __SIZEOF_LONG__ — upstream's own
preferred spelling for exactly this reason, and identical to the branch
being removed on any real x86_64 toolchain.

--- a/arch/x86/include/uapi/asm/bitsperlong.h
+++ b/arch/x86/include/uapi/asm/bitsperlong.h
@@ -2,12 +2,6 @@
 #ifndef __ASM_X86_BITSPERLONG_H
 #define __ASM_X86_BITSPERLONG_H

-#if defined(__x86_64__) && !defined(__ILP32__)
-# define __BITS_PER_LONG 64
-#else
-# define __BITS_PER_LONG 32
-#endif
-
 #include <asm-generic/bitsperlong.h>

 #endif /* __ASM_X86_BITSPERLONG_H */
